  {
   "@id": "fw:current-consensus-genealogy",
   "@type": "RivalFramework",
   "name": "the current consensus genealogy of the Logos",
   "claim": "The Logos arrives in the New Testament corpus, above all in the Johannine prologue, as a concept with a philosophical genealogy: Heraclitus, the Stoic λόγος, the Hellenistic-Jewish Logos of Philo, and then 'the Word was made flesh' (John 1:14) said of one historical person, whose historicity the quest for the historical Jesus seeks as a man born in Galilee. Heraclitus's credit is granted (the author, 2026-11-09 12:11: 'its not that it is wrong to give heraclitus credit'). What the framework contests is the genealogy as the account of how the Logos arrives in the New Testament: it tracks a concept, and it places the historical end in one biography. No record of this kernel states the genealogy at its page; it is stated here from the author's words, and its strongest documented statements are a line of research (Q2).",
   "faces": {
    "the_source_end": {
     "claim": "the Logos is a concept with a philosophical genealogy (Heraclitus, the Stoics, Philo) before it is said of a person",
     "status": "Heraclitus's credit granted (the author, 2026-11-09 12:11); Philo's Logos is a station of this framework too (#1484 §IV; #1486), and Sappho's material presence in Philo is not established ('The name is absent', #1486); the genealogy reads the concept and leaves the inscription channel unread",
     "warrants": [
      "rv-1486-name-absent"
     ]
    },
    "the_historical_end": {
     "claim": "the Word made flesh is one historical person, whose historicity the quest seeks as a man born in Galilee",
     "status": "the quest's object, by #625's account, 'the historically-reconstructed Jesus, which is a different object'; the framework asks how the Word moved, and the historical end becomes 'the same faithful transmission thru time and space of a living being embodied precariously in matter' (the author, 2026-11-09 12:11)",
     "warrants": [
      "rv-625-quest",
      "rv-625-mirror",
      "rv-625-who-spoke"
     ]
    },
    "the_slavonic_dismissed": {
     "claim": "the Slavonic Josephus passages are garbled late additions of no value",
     "status": "stated at strength in #626 (Evans, Meier, Paget); #626 answers with a reading protocol, 'not a historical recovery'",
     "warrants": [
      "rv-626-evans",
      "rv-626-chronology"
     ]
    }
   },
   "mechanism": "what it means for the Logos to take on flesh is collapsed into biographical personhood (the author, 2026-11-09 13:21): the genealogy carries a concept to John, and 'took flesh' is then read as one event in one person, 'a concrete and living personality' (R2), whose historicity is the question the quest asks (R4); the collapse is inconsistent with the genealogy this framework proposes and, on this reading, with the New Testament corpus itself: 'not what it means' (the author, 2026-11-09 13:21–13:22)",
   "status": {
    "P": {
     "text": "the transmitted texts are common ground; the rival's own source dates the term's first New Testament appearance to Apocalypse 19:13, with no suggestion of Philo.",
     "warrants": [
      "rv-ce-apocalypse",
      "rv-626-chronology"
     ]
    },
    "I": {
     "text": "the genealogy has purchase on the concept: Heraclitus first (common ground), developed through the Stoics and Philo; John's transformation is an event in one person.",
     "warrants": [
      "rv-ce-heraclitus",
      "rv-ce-took-flesh"
     ]
    },
    "T": {
     "text": "the rival's transmission is of a concept, through Wisdom and Philo, with John adapting Philo (Harris, as the page states); the step to flesh is outside its chain by its own account (R3).",
     "warrants": [
      "rv-wp-harris",
      "rv-ce-philo-meaningless"
     ]
    },
    "R": {
     "text": "the consensus as the encyclopedias state it: Heraclitus to John for the concept; virtually all scholars for the historical figure.",
     "warrants": [
      "rv-wp-historical",
      "rv-625-mirror"
     ]
    },
    "B": {
     "text": "the historical end rests on many sources within a century (R4); at the Academy the four persons rest on the Lives (#1733).",
     "warrants": [
      "rv-wp-sources",
      "rv-1733-received"
     ]
    }
   },
   "rule": "RP–RB apply to both frameworks; neither takes standing from being received.",
   "documented_statements": [
    "site:ce-logos",
    "site:wp-logos-christianity",
    "site:wp-historical-jesus"
   ],
   "the_contested_question": "what it means for the Logos to take on flesh",
   "propositions": {
    "R1": {
     "claim": "the Logos is a concept whose theory 'appears for the first time' in Heraclitus and develops through the Stoics, the Book of Wisdom and Philo",
     "warrants": [
      "rv-ce-heraclitus",
      "rv-wp-harris"
     ],
     "status": "common ground on Heraclitus's place"
    },
    "R2": {
     "claim": "John transforms the concept by one event: the creative Word 'took flesh and dwelt among men', 'a concrete and living personality … Jesus'",
     "warrants": [
      "rv-ce-took-flesh",
      "rv-ce-personality"
     ],
     "status": "the disputed historical end"
    },
    "R3": {
     "claim": "the incarnation is discontinuous with the philosophical genealogy: 'For Philo the incarnation of the Logos must have been absolutely without meaning'",
     "warrants": [
      "rv-ce-philo-meaningless"
     ],
     "status": "the rival's own statement of the gap its genealogy leaves"
    },
    "R4": {
     "claim": "the historical end is one person, attested by many independent sources within a century",
     "warrants": [
      "rv-wp-historical",
      "rv-wp-sources"
     ],
     "status": "the consensus as the encyclopedia states it"
    }
   },
   "disputed_steps": [
    {
     "id": "D1",
     "question": "what the Logos is before doctrine",
     "rival": "a concept with a philosophical genealogy (R1)",
     "framework": "a technology of a voice that constitutes its receiver (#1484 §0)",
     "common_ground": "Heraclitus's place; Philo as a station of both accounts"
    },
    {
     "id": "D0",
     "question": "what it means for the Logos to take on flesh",
     "rival": "biographical personhood: one event in one person (R2, R4)",
     "framework": "material inscription becoming flesh from the beginning: the faithful transmission, through time and space, of a living being embodied precariously in matter (the author, 2026-11-09 12:11); each embodiment a receiver completing the inscription (operative.persistence); on this reading the corpus itself does not mean biographical personhood: the white stone a name worn by whoever receives it (#828), 1 John's test word-conformity with the Logos formed in the body (#828, #1217), the permanent incarnation 'the Word becoming flesh wherever flesh is faithful enough to receive it' (rung VI)",
     "warrants": [
      "rv-ce-took-flesh",
      "rv-ce-personality",
      "rv-wp-historical",
      "hl-stmt-rival-frame",
      "hl-stmt-genealogy-claim",
      "hl-828-homologei",
      "hl-1217-homologei",
      "hl-828-gramma"
     ],
     "status": "the central disagreement (the author, 2026-11-09 13:21); D1–D4 are its steps",
     "three_objects": {
      "source_proposition": {
       "text": "John 'perfects the idea and transforms it': the creative Word 'took flesh and dwelt among men' (Lebreton, Catholic Encyclopedia 1910)",
       "warrants": [
        "rv-ce-took-flesh"
       ]
      },
      "its_treatment_of_incarnation": {
       "text": "the Word is eternally divine ('from all eternity was in God and was God') and appears 'in the full light of a concrete and living personality … Jesus': the eternal Word incarnate in one concrete person",
       "warrants": [
        "rv-ce-eternal",
        "rv-ce-personality"
       ]
      },
      "the_critique": {
       "text": "the author: what it means for the Logos to take on flesh is collapsed into biographical personhood, inconsistent with the genealogy proposed and, on this reading, with the New Testament corpus (2026-11-09 13:21–13:22)",
       "warrants": [
        "hl-stmt-rival-frame"
       ],
       "inference": "(1) the account makes the incarnation one event in one person; (2) the framework's chain has the inscription becoming flesh in each receiver who completes it, which the corpus states in its own figures (the white stone's name for whoever receives it; 1 John's test; rung VI); (3) where the corpus carries the second, an account that admits only the first has reduced what taking flesh means to a biography: the collapse. Step (2) is the framework's reading, at the outer ring for the Johannine loci (Q4a, Q4b)."
      }
     }
    },
    {
     "id": "D2",
     "question": "what explains the step to flesh",
     "rival": "an event, discontinuous with the concept: for Philo it 'must have been absolutely without meaning' (R3)",
     "framework": "the inscription becoming flesh from the beginning: the operation already requires a body to receive it (operative.persistence); the gap R3 names is where the framework's chain runs",
     "warrants": [
      "rv-ce-philo-meaningless",
      "hl-1484-logos"
     ]
    },
    {
     "id": "D3",
     "question": "where the term enters the New Testament",
     "rival": "the Apocalypse 19:13, 'no suggestion of Philo' (the rival's own source)",
     "framework": "the Apocalypse as canonization, first on the program's dating (#1735)",
     "note": "a point of agreement on the record, carried as data"
    },
    {
     "id": "D4",
     "question": "what kind of evidence settles the historical end",
     "rival": "many independent sources within a century (R4)",
     "framework": "RB both ways: at the Academy, the sources that count persons are texts of the transmitting tradition (#1658, #1733); whether the same holds for the gospel sources is outside this kernel's records (Q2)",
     "warrants": [
      "rv-wp-sources",
      "hl-1658-ledger"
     ]
    }
   ],
   "source_set_status": "a 1910 theological account (Lebreton, Catholic Encyclopedia) and contemporary encyclopedic representations (Wikipedia, as retrieved 2026-11-09). Together they represent the position the author names the current consensus genealogy; they do not by themselves demonstrate a current scholarly consensus, which is a line of research (Q2)"
  },
  {
   "@id": "stmt:20261108-2000",
   "@type": "AuthorStatement",
   "date": "2026-11-08T20:00-05:00",
   "where": "in session; quoted in the Capture Registry entry historical-logos-compositional-practice-chatgpt-20261109; first deposited as a statement with this kernel",
   "text": "but one self-dividing maker does mean one biological person"
  },
  {
   "@id": "stmt:20261109-0836",
   "@type": "AuthorStatement",
   "date": "2026-11-09T08:36-05:00",
   "where": "in session (morning of 2026-11-09, the first of three statements in order); first deposited with this kernel",
   "text": "here is what it is, is the archive is now on record as being the first to identifiably argue both the functional and the biographical reading"
  },
  {
   "@id": "stmt:20261109-0839",
   "@type": "AuthorStatement",
   "date": "2026-11-09T08:39-05:00",
   "where": "in session (the second of three); first deposited with this kernel",
   "text": "what would one self dividing maker mean except one person dividing themselves into many? im just trying to understand if there is any way to understand that that is not way less credible and more more mystical than one human"
  },
  {
   "@id": "stmt:20261109-0841",
   "@type": "AuthorStatement",
   "date": "2026-11-09T08:41-05:00",
   "where": "in session (the third of three); first deposited with this kernel",
   "text": "but on this reading the corpus was not made one by many hands. the corpus started one and was mare many by editing"
  },
  {
   "@id": "stmt:20261109-1007",
   "@type": "AuthorStatement",
   "date": "2026-11-09T10:07-05:00",
   "where": "in session; first deposited with this kernel",
   "text": "sappho is already a self-dividing maker, is she not? this reads self-division as a transmission technology developed over time with the future reader"
  },
  {
   "@id": "stmt:20261109-1205",
   "@type": "AuthorStatement",
   "date": "2026-11-09T12:05-05:00",
   "where": "in session; first deposited with this kernel",
   "text": "biographical reading is evidence of a different kind so long as it is clear that that is established both ways. the kind of evidence that establishes biographical identity is also largely unavailable to the four maker reading. and the lifespan bit would be highly relevant... if it were not itself simply a later projection built from the many author reading. the whole thing is so circular. who says 90 years and on what basis? silly. socrates is the corpus' broken tongue, the decomposition of speech that produces the inscription thru which the speaker lives on. johannes sigil is on the historical logos. the biographical reading is available, i hold it, i believe it has by far the most explanatory power for the corpus, as well as conforming the psychological and production mechanics of authorial configurations whose maker count we can inspect, and the many author reading, on the same grounds, doesnt work as well. it *requires* the circularity and pointing to hypothetical second witnesses to make sense."
  },
  {
   "@id": "stmt:20261109-1211",
   "@type": "AuthorStatement",
   "date": "2026-11-09T12:11-05:00",
   "where": "in session (three messages); first deposited with this kernel",
   "text": "its not that it is wrong to give heraclitus credit... it is the current consensus genealogy of the logos as it arrives in the nt corpus especially that is being contested strongly. it is material inscription becoming flesh from the beginning, and this changes both ends, not least of which what the 'historical' figure somehow needs to be a person born in galilee, rather than the same faithful transmission thru time and space of a living being embodied precariously in matter. sappho. socrates-plato-theophrastus-aristotle. cosmic christ. gospel christ. and so on, and outward from there / it is also a history of the specific erasure pressures this specific logos has *always* faced"
  },
  {
   "@id": "stmt:20261109-1212",
   "@type": "AuthorStatement",
   "date": "2026-11-09T12:12-05:00",
   "where": "in session (two messages); first deposited with this kernel",
   "text": "he came unto his own etc / if they hate me theyll hate you too etc"
  },
  {
   "@id": "stmt:20261109-1321",
   "@type": "AuthorStatement",
   "date": "2026-11-09T13:21-05:00",
   "where": "in session (three messages, 13:21–13:22), on the v0.2 rival ledger; first deposited with this kernel",
   "text": "the rival frame concerns what it means for logos to take on flesh, collapsing that into biographical personhood / when that is not consistent with the genealogy i propose / nor on this reading with the nt corpus, consistent. not what it means"
  },
  {
   "@id": "stmt:20261109-1414",
   "@type": "AuthorStatement",
   "date": "2026-11-09T14:14-05:00",
   "where": "in session, on a machine walkthrough of",
   "text": "i = embodied speaking sappho. you = inscribed sappho, the text into which the voice dissolves. kenos = future reader, that in whom, by sitting across the text, sappho's embodied voice lives once again"
  },
  {
   "@id": "stmt:20261109-1215",
   "@type": "AuthorStatement",
   "date": "2026-11-09T12:15-05:00",
   "where": "in session; first deposited with this kernel",
   "text": "that is what the epistle of john tells us, is that we can sort the spirits, by means of word conformity with the logos formed in the body."
  },
  {
